UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 7: Improvisation and acting skills', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A freeze frame is a still picture that shows the relationship between the characters in one moment. The audience should be able to tell who wants something and who is holding it back before anyone speaks. Space, level, and eye line do that work. Sam low at the shelf and the librarian standing with the stamp held back shows asking and withholding. A caption under the freeze must name the objective, not only the place. ''Sam wants the trolley opened again'' is a caption. ''This is the library'' is only a label. You can draw stick figures or describe the pose in sentences. Building the freeze with your body is optional, agreed first, and done with an adult nearby. No one has to be lifted, grabbed, or put in a pose that feels unsafe.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSam kneels at the low shelf while the librarian holds the date stamp away from the card. Which caption names the objectives in this freeze, rather than only the place?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSam wants the trolley opened again, and the librarian wants the room cleared before the bus. The caption names both wants, so a viewer knows the relationship and not just the furniture. The kneeling and the withheld stamp already show who is asking and who is delaying. A caption that only says ''In the library'' could fit any pose and does not name an objective.\n\nWATCH FOR\nA caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective. A funny face with no decision in it has the same problem. The still picture needs a want. Repair the caption by naming what one character wants from the other in this exact moment.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDescribe a freeze of two teammates and a ball on the sideline, and write a caption that names one player''s objective. Include levels, eye line, and the space between them. Do not use the library or the stamp. The caption must say what someone wants, not only where they are. Acting the freeze is optional, with agreement and an adult nearby. The written pose and caption are the evidence.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Drama","learning_intention":"A freeze frame is a still picture that shows the relationship between the characters in one moment. The audience should be able to tell who wants something and who is holding it back before anyone speaks. Space, level, and eye line do that work. Sam low at the shelf and the librarian standing with the stamp held back shows asking and withholding. A caption under the freeze must name the objective, not only the place. ''Sam wants the trolley opened again'' is a caption. ''This is the library'' is only a label. You can draw stick figures or describe the pose in sentences. Building the freeze with your body is optional, agreed first, and done with an adult nearby. No one has to be lifted, grabbed, or put in a pose that feels unsafe.","lesson_number":7,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DR4-MAK-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Describe a freeze of two teammates and a ball on the sideline, and write a caption that names one player''s objective. Include levels, eye line, and the space between them. Do not use the library or the stamp. The caption Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using freeze on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective.. Correction: Sam wants the trolley opened again, and the librarian wants the room cleared before the bus.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective. A funny face with no decision in it has the same problem. The still picture needs a want. Repair the caption by naming what one character wants from the other in this exact moment. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating freeze on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with freeze, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about freeze with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for freeze. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for freeze. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sam wants the trolley opened again, and the librarian wants the room cleared before the bus","A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work"],"question":"Sam kneels at the low shelf while the librarian holds the date stamp away from the card. Which caption names the objectives in this freeze, rather than only the place?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for freeze; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sam wants the trolley opened again, and the librarian wants the room cleared before the bus","Careful use of relationship with clear evidence","A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective","A freeze frame is a still picture that shows the relationship between the characters in one moment"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with freeze?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named relationship, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name relationship only, without linking it to freeze","Apply freeze to new material and name the relationship used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective"],"question":"Which response best applies freeze with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on freeze. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective","Sam wants the trolley opened again, and the librarian wants the room cleared before the bus","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing freeze?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for freeze","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about freeze?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A freeze frame is a still picture that shows the relationship between) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Sam kneels at the low shelf while the librarian holds the date stamp back, and the caption names both objectives","A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about freeze?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for freeze?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective","Name relationship only, with no linked example","Describe a freeze of two teammates and a ball on the sideline, and write a caption that names one player''s objective"],"question":"Which independent response best applies freeze to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about freeze with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating freeze on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using freeze on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for freeze. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with freeze, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective.. Correction: Sam wants the trolley opened again, and the librarian wants the room cleared before the bus.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-drama-y9-w04-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Drama 7–10 (2023) outcome DR4-MAK-01. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Scenes are original. Physical work is optional, consented, and adult-supervised.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise freeze.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use freeze with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify relationship and explain why it matters.","I can practise caption independently on a new prompt.","I can notice objective needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A freeze frame is a still picture that shows the relationship between the characters in one moment.","options":["A freeze frame is a still picture that shows the relationship between the characters in one moment","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about freeze?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSam kneels at the low shelf while the librarian holds the date stamp back, and the caption names both objectives. Pose: Sam is low, one hand toward the empty trolley, eyes up. The librarian is standing, stamp paused, eyes on the clock. There is a clear gap between them. Nobody is touching. Caption: ''Sam wants one more minute of searching; the librarian wants the room empty for the bus.'' A second freeze, after the guide is found, changes the relationship: both stand at the same level, the stamp comes forward, and the caption says Sam wants the new date and the librarian wants the card finished.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSam kneels at the low shelf while the librarian holds the date stamp away from the card. Which caption names the objectives in this freeze, rather than only the place?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSam wants the trolley opened again, and the librarian wants the room cleared before the bus. The caption names both wants, so a viewer knows the relationship and not just the furniture. The kneeling and the withheld stamp already show who is asking and who is delaying. A caption that only says ''In the library'' could fit any pose and does not name an objective.","explain":"A freeze frame is a still picture that shows the relationship between the characters in one moment. The audience should be able to tell who wants something and who is holding it back before anyone speaks. Space, level, and eye line do that work. Sam low at the shelf and the librarian standing with the stamp held back shows asking and withholding. A caption under the freeze must name the objective, not only the place. ''Sam wants the trolley opened again'' is a caption. ''This is the library'' is only a label. You can draw stick figures or describe the pose in sentences. Building the freeze with your body is optional, agreed first, and done with an adult nearby. No one has to be lifted, grabbed, or put in a pose that feels unsafe.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective. A funny face with no decision in it has the same problem. The still picture needs a want. Repair the caption by naming what one character wants from the other in this exact moment. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A freeze pose with Sam low and the stamp held back, showing the relationship.","caption":"The still picture shows who wants what.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Freeze</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Kneeling, stamp held back</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Relationship</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Asking and withholding</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Sam kneels at the low shelf while the librarian holds the date stamp back, and the caption names both objectives. Pose:  Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Sam kneels at the low shelf while the librarian holds the date stamp back, and the caption names both objectives. Pose:  Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about freeze?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSam kneels at the low shelf while the librarian holds the date stamp back, and the caption names both objectives. Pose: Sam is low, one hand toward the empty trolley, eyes up. The librarian is standing, stamp paused, eyes on the clock. There is a clear gap between them. Nobody is touching. Caption: ''Sam wants one more minute of searching; the librarian wants the room empty for the bus.'' A second freeze, after the guide is found, changes the relationship: both stand at the same level, the stamp comes forward, and the caption says Sam wants the new date and the librarian wants the card finished. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"A caption that names the objective beside a caption that only names the place.","caption":"The caption should name what the character wants.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Caption</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Wants the trolley reopened</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Weak caption</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">This is the library</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about freeze?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective. A funny face with no decision in it has the same problem. The still picture needs a want. Repair the caption by naming what one character wants from the other in this exact moment. Here is a more helpful example: Sam kneels at the low shelf while the librarian holds the date stamp back, and the caption names both objectives. Pose: Sam is low, one hand toward the empty trolley, eyes up. The librarian is standing, stamp paused, eyes on the clock. There is a clear gap between them. Nobody is touching. Caption: ''Sam wants one more minute of searching; the librarian wants the room empty for the bus.'' A second freeze, after the guide is found, changes the relationship: both stand at the same level, the stamp comes forward, and the caption says Sam wants the new date and the librarian wants the card finished.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSam wants the trolley opened again, and the librarian wants the room cleared before the bus. The caption names both wants, so a viewer knows the relationship and not just the furniture. The kneeling and the withheld stamp already show who is asking and who is delaying. A caption that only says ''In the library'' could fit any pose and does not name an objective.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. A caption that only names the place, such as the library, hides the relationship and the objective. A funny face with no decision in it has the same problem. The still picture needs a want. Repair the caption by naming what one character wants from the other in this exact moment. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about freeze?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA freeze frame is a still picture that shows the relationship between the characters in one moment. The audience should be able to tell who wants something and who is holding it back before anyone speaks. Space, level, and eye line do that work. Sam low at the shelf and the librarian standing with the stamp held back shows asking and withholding. A caption under the freeze must name the objective, not only the place. ''Sam wants the trolley opened again'' is a caption. ''This is the library'' is only a label. You can draw stick figures or describe the pose in sentences. Building the freeze with your body is optional, agreed first, and done with an adult nearby. No one has to be lifted, grabbed, or put in a pose that feels unsafe. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 7: Improvisation and acting skills","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Improvisation and acting skills","week":5,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nSam kneels at the low shelf while the librarian holds the date stamp back, and the caption names both objectives. Pose: Sam is low, one hand toward the empty trolley, eyes up. The librarian is standing, stamp paused, eyes on the clock. There is a clear gap between them. Nobody is touching. Caption: ''Sam wants one more minute of searching; the librarian wants the room empty for the bus.'' A second freeze, after the guide is found, changes the relationship: both stand at the same level, the stamp comes forward, and the caption says Sam wants the new date and the librarian wants the card finished.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A freeze pose with Sam low and the stamp held back, showing the relationship.","caption":"The still picture shows who wants what.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Freeze</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Kneeling, stamp held back</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Relationship</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Asking and withholding</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A caption that names the objective beside a caption that only names the place.","caption":"The caption should name what the character wants.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Caption</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Wants the trolley reopened</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Weak caption</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">This is the library</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-drama-y9-w04-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
